<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  ===========================================================================================
  Nop.Web web.config  =  IIS / ASP.NET Core Module (ANCM) HOSTING SHIM ONLY
  Task 7.4 (Requirements 1.4, 4.6, 5.3, 5.8)
  ===========================================================================================

  This file no longer carries any APPLICATION configuration. Everything that used to live here
  moved to appsettings.json, to Program.cs, or to the project file. It is retained only because
  it still holds two genuine IIS SERVER settings (below) that 3.90 expressed here and that IIS
  still honours when the app is hosted behind ANCM. Deleting it entirely is also safe: the Web
  SDK generates a web.config containing the ANCM handler at publish time when none exists.

  The ANCM handler and the <aspNetCore> element are NOT hand written here. The Web SDK's
  publish pipeline injects and maintains them (processPath, arguments, hostingModel), so
  hardcoding them would go stale on every rename or TFM change.

  NOTE THE FILENAME. 3.90 called this file "Web.config" with a capital W. It was RENAMED to the
  all lowercase "web.config" by task 7.4, and that is not cosmetic: the SDK's TransformWebConfig
  task looks for the literal name "web.config" in the publish directory before merging the ANCM
  handler into it. On a case sensitive filesystem (any Linux or container build, which is how
  this migration builds  see build-environment.md) "Web.config" is not found, so the task
  publishes the file verbatim WITHOUT the ANCM handler and the resulting IIS deployment fails.
  Verified empirically: publishing with the capital W produced a "Web.config" containing only the
  two elements below and no <aspNetCore> element at all. Windows builds happened to work, which
  is exactly the kind of platform dependent silence this migration records rather than inherits.

  ===========================================================================================
  ELEMENT BY ELEMENT DISPOSITION OF THE LEGACY FILE. Nothing was dropped silently.
  ===========================================================================================

  <configSections>
    NopConfig  section handler ....... System.Configuration.IConfigurationSectionHandler has no
                                       net10.0 counterpart. Task 2.3 turned NopConfig into an
                                       options POCO; task 7.4 authored the matching
                                       "NopConfig" section in appsettings.json. This closes
                                       runtime deferral 4 / 7.2 -4.
    entityFramework section .......... EF6 only. Nop.Data moved to EF Core in task 3.2, which
                                       configures the provider in NopObjectContext.OnConfiguring.
    resizer section .................. REMOVED, as design section 7 requires. ImageResizer has no
                                       net10.0 release; task 4.2 replaced it with
                                       SixLabors.ImageSharp, which is configured in code and has
                                       no configuration section. The <resizer><plugins> element
                                       (PrettyGifs) went with it  ImageSharp's own GIF encoder
                                       performs the palette quantization PrettyGifs provided.

  <appSettings>
    owin:AutomaticAppStartup ......... dropped, there is no OWIN host.
    webpages:Version / webpages:Enabled  dropped, System.Web.WebPages does not exist.
    PreserveLoginUrl ................. dropped, a System.Web forms authentication quirk switch.
    ClientValidationEnabled .......... 3.90 value "true" is ASP.NET Core's default for
                                       MvcViewOptions.HtmlHelperOptions.ClientValidationEnabled,
                                       so it is preserved with no configuration.
    UnobtrusiveJavaScriptEnabled ..... ASP.NET Core emits only unobtrusive data val attributes;
                                       there is no other mode.
    Use_HTTP_CLUSTER_HTTPS,
    Use_HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO,
    ForwardedHTTPheader .............. all three were COMMENTED OUT in 3.90 and are carried over
                                       still commented out, in appsettings.json "appSettings".
                                       The supported replacement is app.UseForwardedHeaders(),
                                       registered by task 7.2 (runtime deferral 11.29).
    (ClearPluginsShadowDirectoryOnStartup was absent here but IS read by PluginManager, so it is
     now stated explicitly in appsettings.json.)

  <NopConfig> .......................... moved verbatim to appsettings.json "NopConfig".

  <system.web>
    customErrors ..................... task 7.2. UseDeveloperExceptionPage in Development,
                                       otherwise UseExceptionHandler serving ~/ErrorPage.htm  the
                                       exact file defaultRedirect named. The nested
                                       <error statusCode="404" redirect="filenotfound.htm"/> is
                                       superseded by
                                       UseStatusCodePagesWithReExecute("/page-not-found"), which
                                       is what the legacy comment on that line said 3.90 really
                                       relied on ("We handle all 404 errors in PageNotFound of
                                       CommonController"). FileNotFound.htm is still served as a
                                       static file so existing links keep working.
    trace ............................ no counterpart. See the "Logging" section of
                                       appsettings.json for the ASP.NET Core equivalent concept.
    compilation ...................... build concern, owned by the SDK.
    authentication / forms ........... moved to appsettings.json "Authentication", bound into
                                       NopAuthenticationConfig by AddNopFramework(IConfiguration).
                                       SECURITY: requireSSL was "false" in 3.90 and RequireSsl is
                                       therefore false. See the warning there.
    pages / namespaces ............... task 7.3, Views/_ViewImports.cshtml.
    httpRuntime ...................... targetFramework is a build concern.
                                       enableVersionHeader="false" removed the X AspNet Version
                                       response header, which ASP.NET Core never emits.
    sessionState (commented out) ..... HARVESTED BEFORE REDUCING THIS FILE, because it was the
                                       only place the Redis session settings existed. 3.90's
                                       commented block was:
                                         mode="Custom" customProvider="MySessionStateStore"
                                         provider Microsoft.Web.Redis.RedisSessionStateProvider
                                         host="localhost" accessKey="" ssl="true"
                                       i.e. a StackExchange style connection string of
                                       "localhost,ssl=true". It was COMMENTED OUT, so 3.90 shipped
                                       with in process session, which is exactly what
                                       AddNopFramework's AddDistributedMemoryCache() +
                                       AddSession() provides. No Redis session store is wired:
                                       doing so would turn a disabled 3.90 feature on by default.
                                       Runtime deferral 36 / 7.1 -1 stays OPEN with the recipe
                                       recorded  see runtime-deferrals.md section 31.

  <system.webServer>
    httpProtocol / customHeaders ..... KEPT BELOW. IIS, not the application, adds X-Powered-By.
    modules .......................... empty in 3.90 (every entry was commented out).
    staticContent clientCache ........ moved to code. Under ANCM every request is forwarded to
                                       the application, so IIS's static handler never runs and
                                       this element would be dead configuration. Reproduced by
                                       NopStaticFilesExtensions as a Cache-Control max-age of
                                       604800 seconds (7 days) on StaticFileOptions.
    staticContent mimeMap ............ likewise moved to code (.otf, .woff, .woff2, .json).
                                       The .bak mapping is DELIBERATELY NOT reproduced  it
                                       existed to serve database backups over HTTP. See the
                                       comment in NopStaticFilesExtensions and deferral 39.
    urlCompression ................... KEPT BELOW. IIS compresses the response after ANCM returns
                                       it, so this still applies.
    validation ....................... validateIntegratedModeConfiguration existed for the
                                       classic managed modules; there are none.
    handlers
      SitemapXml, RobotsTxt,
      MiniProfiler ................... these three existed only to force extensionless paths
                                       through System.Web's UrlRoutingModule. In ASP.NET Core
                                       every path goes through routing, so they vanish with
                                       nothing to replace. (MiniProfiler itself is dropped 
                                       runtime deferrals section 17.8.)
      DenyAccessToPluginDLLs ......... reproduced by the static file middleware: .dll is not in
                                       the content type map and ServeUnknownFileTypes is false,
                                       so it 404s. Additionally, .dll is in
                                       NopStaticFileProvider's denied extension list and the
                                       Plugins directory is outside its allow-list.
      ExtensionlessUrlHandler,
      OPTIONSVerbHandler,
      TRACEVerbHandler ............... System.Web pipeline plumbing with no counterpart.

  <system.serviceModel> ................ WCF service hosting. Task 4.2 replaced the only WCF
                                       consumer (the EU VAT ASMX proxy) with HttpClient.

  <runtime><assemblyBinding>
    bindingRedirect  (16 of them) ..... not a .NET concept; NuGet resolves one version per
                                       package and central package management pins them
                                       (src/Directory.Packages.props).
    probing privatePath="Plugins/bin/"  PluginManager shadow copies plugin assemblies to
                                       ~/Plugins/bin and loads them explicitly, so no probing
                                       path is needed.

  <system.data><DbProviderFactories> ... SQL Server Compact, removed from scope project wide
                                       (design section 10). EfDataProviderManager now throws
                                       NopException for a "sqlce" provider name.

  <entityFramework><providers> ......... EF6 provider registration. EF Core selects its provider
                                       in NopObjectContext.OnConfiguring.

  Web.Debug.config / Web.Release.config  DELETED. XDT transforms are a Web Application Project
                                       feature that the Web SDK does not run, and both files were
                                       unmodified Visual Studio boilerplate (the only live line
                                       was RemoveAttributes(debug) on <compilation>, which no
                                       longer exists).
  ===========================================================================================
-->
<configuration>
  <system.webServer>
    <!--
      3.90: <httpProtocol><customHeaders><remove name="X-Powered-By" /></customHeaders>
      IIS adds this header itself, outside the application, so removing it is still an IIS level
      instruction. Kestrel never adds it, so a Kestrel only deployment needs nothing.
    -->
    <httpProtocol>
      <customHeaders>
        <remove name="X-Powered-By" />
      </customHeaders>
    </httpProtocol>
    <!--
      3.90: <urlCompression doStaticCompression="true" doDynamicCompression="true" />
      Carried over unchanged. IIS applies compression to the response after ANCM hands it back,
      so this element is still live under IIS hosting.

      TWO THINGS TO KNOW.
      1) A Kestrel only deployment (dotnet run, or a container behind a non IIS proxy) gets NO
         compression from this file. ASP.NET Core's response compression middleware was
         deliberately NOT added to Program.cs, because doing so would have forced a choice
         between (a) EnableForHttps=false, its default, which yields no compression at all for
         an HTTPS storefront, and (b) EnableForHttps=true, which reintroduces the BREACH
         exposure that doDynamicCompression="true" already has here. Neither is a decision this
         migration should make silently for every deployment. Terminate compression at the
         reverse proxy, or add UseResponseCompression() with an explicit, reviewed setting.
      2) doDynamicCompression="true" compresses HTML that contains the antiforgery token. That
         was 3.90's shipped configuration and is preserved for faithfulness, but it is the
         BREACH precondition and is worth an explicit decision on an HTTPS only store.
    -->
    <urlCompression doStaticCompression="true" doDynamicCompression="true" />
  </system.webServer>
</configuration>
